{#
    The question pb:confirm asks.

    Put this wherever it will be on every page -- the layout, usually -- and it
    is used in place of the one PyBlade builds for itself. Style it, translate
    it, rearrange it: what PyBlade needs is only the four marks below.

        pb-confirm-dialogue   the <dialog> itself
        pb-confirm-message    where the question is written
        pb-confirm-prompt     the field for .prompt, hidden unless it is asked for
        pb-confirm-accept     the button that carries the action out
        pb-confirm-cancel     the button that does not

    The buttons sit in a <form method="dialog">, so their value is the answer
    and the dialogue closes on its own. A reader who presses Escape or clicks
    the backdrop has answered no.
#}
<dialog pb-confirm-dialogue class="confirm-dialogue">
    <form method="dialog">
        <p pb-confirm-message></p>

        <input pb-confirm-prompt type="text" autocomplete="off" hidden>

        <div class="confirm-dialogue-actions">
            <button value="cancel" pb-confirm-cancel>@trans('Cancel')</button>
            <button value="accept" pb-confirm-accept>@trans('Confirm')</button>
        </div>
    </form>
</dialog>
